course: dictionary
lesson: 14620
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Porte is a French word, so you will not find it as an everyday English word. The words around it give you clues. «Ferme la porte, s'il te plaît» has the word porte in it, and ferme means close, so porte must mean door.
explain: Porte is also a form of the French verb porter, which means carries or wears. «Il porte un chapeau» means he wears a hat. The same spelling can mean different things, so always read the words around it.
explain: In history, «The Porte» was the name Europeans used for the government of the Ottoman Empire. A capital P is a clue that it is a name, not the French word for door.
explain: English words such as portal, porter and portable come from the same old root. Knowing one root can help you guess several words, but check a dictionary when you are unsure.
fact: Porte is a French word meaning door or gate.
fact: «Porte» is also a form of porter, meaning carries or wears: «il porte» means he carries or he wears.
fact: «The Porte» was the name used in Europe for the government of the Ottoman Empire.
fact: In Paris, some old city gates are named «Porte de ...».
fact: English words portal, porter and portable share the same root as porte.
exercise: «Ferme la porte, s'il te plaît.» In French, what does porte mean? | *A door or gate | A window | A chair | A bed
exercise: «Il porte un chapeau rouge.» What does porte mean in this sentence? | *Wears | Door | Sleeps | Opens
exercise: In history, «The Porte» was the name for which government? | Government of Paris | French government | Roman Empire | *Ottoman Empire
test: Which English word comes from the same root as porte? | castle | bicycle | window | *portable
test: «Ahmed read about the Porte in his history book.» Why does Porte have a capital P here? | It is the French word for door | *It is a name for a government | It is a verb meaning carries | It means very old
exam: «Marc porte son sac sur le dos.» In English, what does porte mean here? | *Carries or wears | Ottoman government | Portable | Door or gate
